## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to construct precise 3-valued assignments with exact unknown counts under Kleene semantics, along with a practical backtracking strategy for constraint satisfaction.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- Exact unknown count in constraint satisfaction
- Propagation and backtracking in three-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  return v === 'T' ? 'F' : v === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U';
}

function imp(a, b) {
  return not(a) === 'T' ? b : not(a);
}

function evaluate(expr, vars) {
  if (Array.isArray(expr)) {
    const [op, left, right] = expr;
    const l = evaluate(left, vars);
    const r = evaluate(right, vars);
    if (op === 'and') return min(l, r);
    if (op === 'or') return max(l, r);
    if (op === 'imp') return imp(l, r);
  }
  return vars[expr];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic in a realistic policy-engine style, including short-circuit semantics and propagation.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: ¬ flips T/F, U→U; ∧/∨ follow min/max with T>U>F; A→B as ¬A ∨ B.
- Short-circuit evaluation in rule engines.
- Backtracking propagation for constraint satisfaction in a 3-valued setting.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(a){ return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ /* min/max with T>U>F */ return (a === 'T' || b === 'T') ? 'T' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ return (a === 'F' || b === 'F') ? 'F' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = kleeneNot(a)
  return kleeneOr(na, b)
}
```

